4. Formal Redundancy and Capacity Model

4.1 Available Thrust Model

Let $\mathcal{O}(t)$ be the set of operational engines at time $t$. Available thrust is:

F_{\rm available}(t) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{O}(t)} F_i(t)

Margin of maneuver is defined as:

M(t) = F_{\rm available}(t) - F_{\rm required}(t)

4.2 Functional Condition

M(t) \geq 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{maneuver potentially achievable}

M(t) < 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{insufficient propulsive capacity}

Methodological precision: This condition is necessary but not sufficient. Attitude control, structural integrity, fluid dynamics, and design margins remain determining factors. It does not claim to explain all actual dynamics.

4.3 Capacity State

The vehicle capacity state at time $t$ is represented by the quadruplet:

R(t) = \big[ \mathcal{O}(t), \, F_{\rm available}(t), \, F_{\rm required}(t), \, M(t) \big]

4.4 Application to Flight Phases (SpaceX Data)

Flight / Phase $n_{\rm total}$ $n_{\rm op}$ Consequence
Flight 14 Ship (ascent) 6 5 Orbit achieved, 5 engines burned longer than planned
Flight 14 Ship (orbital insertion) 1 sea-level Raptor 1 Orbital insertion performed
Flight 14 Booster (boostback) 33 31 Boostback completed
Flight 14 Booster (landing burn) 13 11 → 5 → 3 Controlled splashdown in the Gulf
Flight 13 Booster (landing burn) 13 10 → 8 → 5 Hard splashdown

Sources: SpaceX, "Starship Flight 14," September 28, 2026 — S1. Flight 13 Booster sequence: 10 engines relit initially, 8 successfully reached mainstage, down-selected to 5 before impact.

