5. Detailed Causal Analysis — Audit E01 to E17

5.1 E01 — SN8

Vehicle: Ship SN8

Phase: Propulsive landing

Status: REPORTED (manufacturer statement, no closed investigation)

Source: Elon Musk, statement of December 9, 2020 — S2 (social media statement, not formal S1 document)

Causal chain:

P_{\text{header}} \downarrow \rightarrow \dot{m}_{\text{propellants}} \downarrow \rightarrow F_{\text{thrust}} \downarrow \rightarrow v_{\text{impact}} \uparrow \rightarrow \text{Destruction}

Source quote: "Fuel header tank pressure was low during landing burn, causing touchdown velocity to be high & RUD."

Status note: The FAA directed SpaceX to investigate SN8, but no causal investigation report was published. The attribution to header tank pressure rests on the manufacturer's statement alone.

5.2 E02 — SN9

Vehicle: Ship SN9

Phase: Landing burn

Status: REPORTED (functional event)

Source: SpaceX — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Relight failure} \rightarrow F_{\text{available}} \downarrow \rightarrow a_{\text{braking}} \downarrow \rightarrow \text{Impact}

Source quote: "During the landing flip maneuver, one of the Raptor engines did not relight and caused SN9 to land at high speed and experience a RUD."

Precision: "Non-relight" is a functional symptom. The physical cause of the non-relight is not publicly documented in a formal investigation. The functional event is established; the material cause is not. The FAA cleared SpaceX for SN10 launch but did not publish a causal finding.

5.3 E03 — SN10

Vehicle: Ship SN10

Phase: Landing

Status: CAUSE PROPOSED BY MANUFACTURER (probable)

Source: Elon Musk, statement of March 10, 2021 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{He ingestion}_{\text{probable}} \rightarrow F_{\text{thrust}} \downarrow \rightarrow \text{Hard landing} \rightarrow \text{Fire} \rightarrow \text{Delayed destruction}

Source quote: "SN10 engine was low on thrust due (probably) to partial helium ingestion from fuel header tank. Impact of 10m/s crushed legs & part of skirt."

Precision: The term "probable" is retained. This is not an FAA investigation conclusion. The matrix reflects this status as "CAUSE PROPOSED" and not "CAUSE ESTABLISHED."

5.4 E04 — SN11

Vehicle: Ship SN11

Phase: Flight and landing burn

Status: REPORTED (manufacturer reconstruction)

Source: Elon Musk, statement of April 5, 2021 — S2 (social media statement)

Causal chain:

\text{CH}_4 \text{ leak} \rightarrow \text{Fire} \rightarrow \text{Avionics damage} \rightarrow \text{Hard start} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Source quote: "A (relatively) small CH4 leak led to fire on engine 2 & fried part of avionics, causing hard start attempting landing burn in CH4 turbopump."

Precision: This is a historical reconstruction by the manufacturer, not a modern investigation report. The hard start and the leak are part of a complex chain. The formulation is retained with caution.

5.5 E05 — Flight 3

Vehicle: Super Heavy

Phase: Boostback → landing burn

Status: CAUSE ESTABLISHED

Source: SpaceX, update of May 24, 2024 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{LOX filter blockage} \rightarrow P_{\text{inlet}} \downarrow \rightarrow \text{6 engines shut down early during boostback} \rightarrow \text{7 engines commanded for landing, only 2 reached mainstage} \rightarrow \text{Booster broke apart at ~462 m altitude}

Source quote: "The most likely root cause for the early boostback burn shutdown was determined to be continued filter blockage where liquid oxygen is supplied to the engines, leading to a loss of inlet pressure in engine oxygen turbopumps."

Precision: The filter blockage explains the early boostback shutdown. The destruction occurred at landing, with only 2 of 13 engines reaching mainstage ignition. Contact was lost at approximately 462 meters altitude over the Gulf of Mexico. A similar filter blockage problem occurred during Flight 2 in November 2023. SpaceX implemented hardware changes for Flight 4 to improve propellant filtration.

5.6 E06 — Flight 7

Vehicle: Starship Ship

Phase: Powered flight

Status: CAUSE ESTABLISHED (SpaceX investigation supervised and accepted by FAA)

Source: FAA, March 31, 2025 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Excessive vibrations} \rightarrow \sigma_{\text{stresses}} \uparrow \rightarrow \text{Propulsion failure} \rightarrow \text{Leak} \rightarrow \text{Fire} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Source quote: "The final mishap report cites the probable root cause for the loss of the Starship vehicle was stronger than anticipated vibrations during flight led to increased stress on, and failure of, the hardware in the propulsion system."

Precision: The FAA supervised and accepted the conclusions of the investigation conducted by SpaceX, and did not conduct the technical analysis itself. SpaceX identified 11 corrective actions, verified by the FAA before Flight 8.

5.7 E07 — Flight 8

Vehicle: Starship Ship

Phase: Ascent

Status: CAUSE ESTABLISHED (SpaceX investigation supervised and accepted by FAA)

Source: FAA, June 12, 2025 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Raptor failure} \rightarrow \text{Inadvertent mixing} \rightarrow \text{Ignition} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Source quote: "The final mishap report cites the probable root cause for the loss of the Starship vehicle as a hardware failure in one of the raptor engines that resulted in inadvertent propellant mixing and ignition."

Precision: SpaceX specified that this was a hardware failure of a central Raptor engine, details of which were not disclosed. The failure allowed "inadvertent propellant mixing and ignition" that caused the loss of the Raptor, then the shutdown of the other two central Raptors and one RVac. The causes of Flight 7 and Flight 8 are distinct.

5.8 E08 — Flight 9 Ship

Vehicle: Starship Ship

Phase: Flight

Status: PROBABLE ROOT CAUSE ESTABLISHED (closed investigation, accepted by FAA)

Source: FAA, August 15, 2025 — S1

Simplified causal chain:

\text{Fuel component failure} \rightarrow \text{Degraded control} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Detailed chain (SpaceX):

\text{Pressurization diffuser failure} \rightarrow \text{CH}_4 \text{ leak} \rightarrow \text{Pressure loss} \rightarrow \text{Attitude control loss} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Source quote: "The final mishap report cites the probable root cause for the loss of the Starship vehicle as a failure of a fuel component."

Detailed mechanism (SpaceX): The component concerned is the diffuser of the main fuel tank pressurization system, located on the forward dome of the tank in the vehicle's nose. Approximately three minutes after combustion began, sensors detected an increase in methane in the nose, followed by a sudden pressure drop. Liquid methane entered the nose section, causing a significant attitude error. The vehicle became passive and performed a reentry at poor attitude.

Precision: The FAA root cause and the detailed SpaceX mechanism are separated. The regulatory formulation of reference is "failure of a fuel component"; the detailed technical formulation is attributed to the SpaceX report.

5.9 E09 — Flight 9 Booster

Vehicle: Super Heavy

Phase: Return

Status: CAUSE ESTABLISHED (closed investigation) — with test-induced damage exception

Source: SpaceX/FAA — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Transfer component failure} \rightarrow \text{Rupture} \rightarrow \text{Propellant interaction} \rightarrow \text{Loss}

Source quote: "The most probable cause for the failure at landing burn was higher than predicted forces on the booster structure, specifically on the booster's fuel transfer tube. The failure of that transfer tube caused methane and liquid oxygen propellants to mix and ignite."

Critical qualification: The FAA determined that the loss of the Super Heavy booster on Flight 9 is covered by one of the approved test-induced damage exceptions requested by SpaceX for certain flight events and system components. The exceptions include "failure of the booster grid fin system, poor engine initialization during the landing burn, and a hard water impact due to insufficient engine thrust". The FAA evaluated each exception prior to launch approval and verified they met public safety requirements.

Implication: The Flight 9 Booster loss was a planned, pre-approved test outcome, not an ordinary failure. This qualification must be considered when analyzing the causal chain.

5.10 E10 — Ship 36

Vehicle: Ship 36

Category: Ground test — static fire

Status: CAUSE PROPOSED BY MANUFACTURER (preliminary data, publicly attributed)

Source: SpaceX / Elon Musk, statement of June 19, 2025 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{N}_2 \text{ COPV failure} \rightarrow \text{Rupture} \rightarrow \text{Propellant interaction} \rightarrow \text{Ignition} \rightarrow \text{Destruction}

Source quote: "Initial analysis indicates the failure of a COPV (composite overwrapped pressure vessel) containing gaseous nitrogen, located in the nosecone of the ship."

Detailed attribution: Elon Musk stated: "Preliminary data suggests that a nitrogen COPV in the payload bay failed below its proof pressure." The failure occurred during propellant loading for a six-engine static fire at the Massey's Test Site near Starbase, Texas, on June 18, 2025. Research & Development World characterized the event as demonstrating that "even known failure modes can resurface in new contexts".

Precision: The status is "preliminary" in the manufacturer's own language. No confirmation by formal investigation has been published as of September 29, 2026. The COPV failure is publicly attributed but not formally established.

5.11 E11 — Flight 12 Booster

Vehicle: Super Heavy V3

Phase: Ascent then return

Status: PROBABLE ROOT CAUSE ESTABLISHED (closed investigation, accepted by FAA)

Source: FAA, July 13, 2026 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Thermal effects} + \text{Alarm configuration} \rightarrow \text{Propulsion degradation} \rightarrow \text{Relight capacity} \downarrow \rightarrow \text{Hard splashdown}

Source quote: "The final mishap report cites two most probable root causes for the loss of the Super Heavy booster as heat effects on propulsion system components during the ascent and erroneous engine alarm system settings."

Precision: The FAA announced the closure on Monday, July 13, 2026, accepting SpaceX's four corrective actions, including vehicle hardware and software configuration updates. The booster plunged into the Gulf of Mexico at high speed, exploding on impact. The FAA confirmed no injuries or public property damage.

5.12 E12 — Flight 12 Ship

Vehicle: Starship V3

Phase: Ascent

Status: OBSERVED + REPORTED

Source: SpaceX — S1

Sequence:

\text{Raptor Vacuum shutdown} \rightarrow |\mathcal{O}| : 6 \rightarrow 5 \rightarrow M(t) > 0 \rightarrow \text{Mission continued}

Classification: Non-fatal anomaly.

Source quote: "One of six Starship upper-stage engines also shut down about half a minute after stage separation."

5.13 E13 — Flight 13 Booster

Vehicle: Super Heavy V3

Phase: Return / landing burn

Status: OBSERVED (root cause not established)

Source: SpaceX, Flight 13 post-flight update of September 15, 2026 — S1; Aviation Week — S2

Causal chain:

C_p = \text{not established} \rightarrow I = \text{insufficient relight capacity (ice-clogging signs reported)} \rightarrow T = \text{hard splashdown}

Observed sequence (SpaceX): "During the terminal phase of the burn, the three center engines showed signs of ice clogging which triggered an early end to the maneuver. The booster went on to attempt a landing burn, with 8 of the 13 planned engines reigniting before the booster made a hard splashdown in the Gulf."

Engine count sequence: Sources indicate 10 engines relit initially during the landing burn attempt, with only 8 successfully reaching mainstage ignition before the booster was down-selected to 5 engines and impacted the water.

Source attribution precision: The ice-clogging quote is from a SpaceX website update posted September 15, 2026, as cited by Aviation Week. This is a manufacturer-reported functional observation, not a formally closed root-cause finding.

Precision: "Incomplete relight" is a functional event, not necessarily the root cause. The physical cause of the relight failure — ice clogging or otherwise — is not established in a formal investigation. SpaceX has implemented hardware modifications to improve filtering to the engines and software changes to enhance relight reliability for Flight 14.

5.14 E14 — Flight 13 Ship

Vehicle: Starship V3 / Ship 40

Phase: Atmospheric return

Status: OBSERVED (established fact)

Source: SpaceX — S1

Observed sequence:

\text{Reentry} \rightarrow \text{Flaps} \rightarrow \text{Flip} \rightarrow \text{3-engine relight} \rightarrow \text{Soft splashdown} \rightarrow \text{Vehicle structurally intact}

Causal importance:

\text{Soft splashdown} \rightarrow \text{Vehicle structurally intact}

\text{Water contact} \nRightarrow \text{Destruction}

Precision: Ship 40 was recovered structurally intact and buoyant, marking the first intact ocean recovery of a Starship upper stage after returning from space. NASASpaceFlight reports that several tiles are missing from the aft section and the bottom rim exhibits damage. The term "structurally intact" is therefore preferred over "intact" to reflect the observed condition.

5.15 E15 and E16 — Flight 14 Ship (Ship 41)

Vehicle: Starship V3 / Ship 41

Status: OBSERVED + REPORTED + CAUSE UNKNOWN

Source: SpaceX, "Starship Flight 14," September 28, 2026 — S1

Sequence 1 (ascent):

C_p^{?} \rightarrow \text{single Raptor Vacuum engine shut down early} \rightarrow |\mathcal{O}| : 6 \rightarrow 5 \rightarrow \text{remaining five engines burned longer than planned} \rightarrow \text{passively safe suborbital trajectory achieved} \rightarrow M(t) > 0 \rightarrow \text{orbital insertion performed with one sea-level Raptor} \rightarrow \text{orbit achieved}

Exact quote (SpaceX): "During Starship's ascent burn, a single Raptor vacuum engine shut down early. Starship still achieved its planned, passively safe suborbital trajectory by burning its remaining five engines for longer than planned to compensate for the loss of thrust. After entering its suborbital trajectory, the flight control team assessed Starship's systems and determined the three sea-level Raptors engines needed for subsequent burns were healthy and gave a 'go' for orbital operations."

Sequence 2 (post-splashdown):

\text{Controlled landing burn with all three sea-level Raptors} \rightarrow \text{splashdown on target} \rightarrow \text{tip-over} \rightarrow \text{residual propellant ignition / destruction}

SpaceX characterisation: The official page does not use the phrase "an outcome SpaceX expected" verbatim, but describes the vehicle as "splashing down on target" followed by the standard post-splashdown sequence.

Mission duration note: The flight lasted approximately 3 hours, with the ship splashing down near Hawaii. The original 10-hour plan was abandoned after the RVac shutdown, with the flight control team deciding to limit the duration spent on orbit "out of an abundance of caution given the engine issue experienced during ascent."

Splashdown softness divergence: CNN reported that "the splashdown wasn't as soft as the one seen in July during the previous flight, causing a massive fireball over the water." Other sources describe it as a "controlled splashdown." The divergence is noted. The term "controlled landing burn" is retained with the caveat that the splashdown was not as soft as Flight 13's.

Separated statuses:

Element Status
Premature RVac shutdown ESTABLISHED
Physical cause of RVac shutdown NOT ESTABLISHED
Post-splashdown destruction ESTABLISHED as observed event
Physical initiation mechanism of the fire (precise role of residual propellants, ignition source, contribution of capsizing) NOT ESTABLISHED

5.16 E17 — Flight 14 Booster

Vehicle: Super Heavy

Status: OBSERVED + REPORTED (post-splashdown FTS)

Source: SpaceX, "Starship Flight 14," September 28, 2026 — S1

Causal chain:

\text{Controlled splashdown} \rightarrow \text{FTS activation} \rightarrow \text{Commanded destruction}

Source quote: "Upon landing burn shutdown, the Super Heavy's flight termination system was triggered in a demonstration of the safety hardware."

Detailed sequence (SpaceX): "Super Heavy performed its directional flip maneuver and relit 31 of the planned 33 Raptor engines for the boostback burn. The booster then reignited 11 of the planned 13 engines for the high-thrust portion of the landing burn before down-selecting to five engines for trajectory fine-tuning, and then down to three engines before splashing down in the Gulf."

Precision: The official SpaceX page explicitly documents the post-splashdown FTS sequence. The status is OBSERVED + REPORTED (S1). The FTS must be distinguished from spontaneous destruction. SpaceX confirms that during the initial climb to space, a single Raptor engine on the booster shut down, but the booster still completed its boostback and landing burns.

