11. Conclusion

The study of Starship losses does not reveal a single cause called "explosion." It reveals a collection of different causal chains.

Documented mechanisms notably include: pressure issues, propellant feed issues, probable gas ingestion, filter blockages, relight failures, hard starts, leaks, Raptor hardware failures, inadvertent propellant mixing, excessive vibrations, thermal effects, pressurization issues, loss of control, loss of thrust, hard splashdown, post-splashdown events, ground destructions, and commanded destructions.

The essential distinction is:

\boxed{\text{Same symptom} \neq \text{Same cause}}

and:

\boxed{\text{Same apparent cause} \neq \text{Same consequence}}

An engine that shuts down may cause the loss of a vehicle in a critical phase. The same type of anomaly may be absorbed by redundancy in another phase. Flight 12 and Flight 14 illustrate this.

Finally, Flight 13 constitutes the decisive observation concerning splashdown:

\boxed{\text{Soft splashdown} \rightarrow \text{Vehicle structurally intact}}

It is therefore impossible to consider water contact as a sufficient cause of explosion.

Flight 14 adds a second observation:

\boxed{\text{Non-fatal engine anomaly}}

then:

\boxed{\text{Controlled splashdown} \rightarrow \text{Post-contact destructive event}}

with a detailed physical cause still not established as of September 29, 2026.

The correct scientific question is therefore no longer: "Why does Starship explode?" but rather: Which specific causal chain led to the loss or destruction of which vehicle, during which phase of which flight, and what level of redundancy remained available when the failure occurred?

This formulation allows causes, intermediate mechanisms, and terminal events to be studied separately, without transforming a spectacular image into a causal explanation. It is less Hollywood, but physics has rarely asked our permission to be boring.

