Appendix B — Extended Technical Glossary

Term Definition Related Section
Attic Non-pressurized zone between the LOX tank bottom and the aft heat shield §3.2
Boostback burn Retrograde burn performed by the booster after stage separation to reverse trajectory toward the launch site or designated splashdown zone §1.1
COPV Composite Overwrapped Pressure Vessel — a pressure vessel consisting of a metal liner wrapped with composite fibers §3.2
Cryogenic fatigue Degradation of materials due to repeated thermal cycling between ambient and cryogenic temperatures §9
Diffuser Component that distributes pressurization gas into a propellant tank §5.8
FTS Flight Termination System — commanded destruction mechanism designed to terminate flight safely §3.2
Grid fin Aerodynamic control surface on the booster used for attitude control during descent §5.9
Hard landing Contact with sufficient kinetic energy to cause structural damage §3.2
Hard splashdown Water contact with sufficient kinetic energy to cause significant damage or immediate destruction §3.2
Hard start Abnormal rocket engine ignition characterized by accumulation of unburned propellants in the combustion chamber, producing sudden overpressure upon ignition §3.2
Header tank Small propellant tank used to supply engines during landing burn when main tanks are not pressurized for engine feed §5.1
Helium ingestion Introduction of helium bubbles into the propellant feed line, reducing fluid density and thus mass flow rate §3.2
Ice clogging Formation of ice in propellant feed lines, potentially obstructing flow to engine inlets §3.2
LOX Liquid Oxygen — oxidizer used in Raptor engines §3.2
Mainstage ignition The point at which a rocket engine reaches full nominal combustion §5.5
Passive reentry Reentry in which the vehicle has lost active attitude control §5.8
Proof pressure The pressure to which a pressure vessel is tested to verify structural integrity, typically 1.5× maximum expected operating pressure §5.10
RVac Raptor Vacuum — Raptor engine version optimized for vacuum operation, with a wider nozzle §3.2
RUD Rapid Unscheduled Disassembly — industry euphemism for catastrophic structural failure §5.1
Slosh Movement of propellant within tanks during vehicle maneuvers §9
Soft splashdown Controlled contact with water, vehicle structurally intact and floating §3.2
Tip-over Post-splashdown capsizing of the vehicle due to instability in water §5.15
Test-induced damage exception Pre-approved FAA determination that certain failures during test flights are acceptable under public safety requirements §5.9

